    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-r/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) From dx/dt = −3 cos2 t sin t [M1] and dy/dt = 3 sin2 t cos t, [A1] dividing and cancelling gives −3 sin2 t cos t/(3 cos2 t sin t) = −sin t/ cos t. Thus the tangent gradient is −tan t. [A1] The normal has gradient cot θ, so y −sin3 θ = cot θ(x −cos3 θ). [A1] Multiply by sin θ: y sin θ −sin4 θ = x cos θ −cos4 θ cos4 θ −sin4 θ = x cos θ −y sin θ Factor the difference of squares as (cos2 θ −sin2 θ)(cos2 θ + sin2 θ) = cos 2θ.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-r/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Thus x cos θ −y sin θ = cos4 θ −sin4 θ = cos 2θ . [A1] (b) At x = 0, −y sin θ = cos 2θ; at y = 0, x cos θ = cos 2θ. Thus the intercepts are cos 2θ/ cos θ and −cos 2θ/ sin θ. [B1] Half the absolute product is cos2 2θ 2 sin θ cos θ = cos 2θ cot 2θ . [M1] [A1] At θ = π/4 both intercepts coincide at the origin and this expression gives zero. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
